From c19c86c38d9ce985305025c274659895ef78eb2f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Vasilii Zolotukhin <vasilii.zolotukhin@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 21:44:00 +1000
Subject: [PATCH 09/10] Mac: the unified strip also works with vertical tabs

With Tab position: Vertical, Chromium shows its vertical tab strip and
the unified strip stayed in the hidden horizontal region, so every
window showed only its own profile's tabs.

With vertical tabs the unified strip becomes a column of rows inside
the vertical region. It covers the tab list and the new tab button under
it, leaving the buttons at the top (collapse, tab groups, tab search) in
place, and draws its own "+" row after the last tab. The rows scroll
with the mouse wheel when they do not fit, the "+" row stays at the
bottom, and a newly active tab scrolls into view. Drag reordering works
along the column, and the collapsed strip shows just the icons.
Switching the tab position in settings rebuilds the strip on the fly.

Chromium keeps the tab position per profile and the collapse state and
width per window. In the group they are shared: changing them in one
window applies to all profiles of the group, and a window joining the
group takes the group's state, so the strip does not change shape when
switching between profiles.

The strip's context menu also lists "New tab in <profile>", and
accessibility clients can press a tab to select it.
